public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
        return result;
    }

    doPermute(nums, 0, result);
    return result;
}

public void doPermute(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
    if (start == nums.length - 1) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(nums)));
        return;
    }

    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        swap(nums, start, i);
        doPermute(nums, start + 1, result);
        swap(nums, start, i);
    }
}
```

The `permute` method initializes an empty list to store the result. It then calls the private `doPermute` method, passing the array, the starting index `0`, and the result list. The `doPermute` method recursively generates all permutations by swapping the current element with each element after it, and recursively calling itself to generate the permutations for the remaining elements. When the length of the permutation is equal to the length of the input array, it adds the current permutation to the result list.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
            return result;
        }
        helper(nums, 0, result);
        return result;
    }

    private void helper(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (start == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.copyOf(nums, nums.length)));
            return;
        }

        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            swap(nums, i, start);
            helper(nums, start + 1, result);
            swap(nums, i, start);
        }
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
        int temp = nums[i];
        nums[i] = nums[j];
        nums[j] = temp;
    }
}
