public boolean splitArraySameAverage(int[] nums) {
    Arrays.sort(nums);
    int totalSum = Arrays.stream(nums).sum();
    int targetSum = totalSum / 2;
    return findSubarray(nums, 0, nums.length - 1, targetSum);
}

private boolean findSubarray(int[] nums, int start, int end, int targetSum) {
    if (end - start + 1 < 2) return false;
    int subSum = 0;
    for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
        subSum += nums[i];
        if (subSum == targetSum) return true;
        if (subSum > targetSum) return false;
        if (i == end) return findSubarray(nums, start, i - 1, targetSum);
    }
    return false;
}
```

***Explanation***  
The `splitArraySameAverage` method first sorts the array to facilitate processing. It then calculates the target sum, which is half of the total sum of the array. The `findSubarray` method uses a helper function to recursively check if there exists a subarray within the given range that can sum up to the target value. If it finds such a subarray, it returns `true`. If the array elements do not satisfy the split condition, it returns `false`.

```java
public boolean splitArraySameAverage(int[] nums) {
    // Calculate the total sum of elements in the array
    int totalSum = Arrays.stream(nums).sum();
    int n = nums.length;

    // Iterate over possible split points
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int leftSum = totalSum - nums[i];
        int rightSum = totalSum;
        int target = leftSum * 2;
        // If the target is greater than leftSum, skip split
        if (target > leftSum) continue;
        // Starting left pointer from i + 1
        for (int l = i + 1; l < n; l++) {
            rightSum -= nums[l];
            // Check if the right sum equals target
            if (rightSum == target) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        // If no split found, reset right sum and continue
        rightSum = totalSum;
    }
    // If still no split found
    return false;
}
